## The Bug

The issue is a classic Go pre-1.22 loop variable capture problem. In Go 1.21, the loop variable `i` is **shared across all iterations** of the `for` loop. Each goroutine closes over the *same* variable `i`, not a copy of its value at the time the goroutine was spawned.

By the time any of the goroutines actually get scheduled and execute `fmt.Println(i)`, the loop has already finished and `i` has been incremented to `5`. So every goroutine prints `5`.

The relevant lines are:

```go
for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {   // i is one shared variable
    ...
    go func() {
        fmt.Println(i)      // all goroutines see the same (final) i
    }()
}
```

## The Fix

Capture `i` as a function parameter so each goroutine receives its own copy of the value at spawn time:

```go
go func(i int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)
}(i)
```

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(i int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(i)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

**Note:** Starting with Go 1.22, the loop variable semantics changed so that each iteration gets its own `i`. If you ever upgrade to 1.22+, your original code would work as expected. But on Go 1.21, the parameter-capture fix is the standard solution.

